#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""One CPU gate for a checkout: cargo workspace tests and the script suite.

  scripts/dev/cpu-gate --task NAME [--jobs N] [--full] [--slow] [--cargo] [--scripts] [--benches] [--skip-live-planners] [--base REV] [--keep]

--jobs is the total worker budget: split evenly between Cargo and pytest when
both run, otherwise given entirely to the requested suite. Default excludes
measured full-plan/cache and live V4 tests and is the merge gate; --full
restores them for release cuts. This includes the installed-example deployment-to-
release_prefill_shape sweep: HF_HOME (or ~/.cache/huggingface) is the default
inventory; CUTEAFD_DEPLOYMENT_TEST_HF_HOME overrides it. Missing required inputs
fail, including pinned checkpoint revisions and the published placement-inputs
share/PROGRAMS.json with sibling lib/fp8 and adjacent expert metadata. CUTEAFD_DEPLOYMENT_TEST_PROGRAMS
overrides manifest discovery; the gate only reads exports, never builds them.
Before taking a gate lease or compiling, the gate computes the checkout's
placement inputs key (fork pin, tree hash and export-source fingerprints, by the
checkout's own exporter code) and points CUTEAFD_DEPLOYMENT_TEST_PROGRAMS and
CUTEAFD_V4_PLACEMENT_INPUTS at the publication recording that key: current when
it matches, else any matching publication in the store, so a branch on an older
base tests against its own compiled facts. With no match it fails in seconds,
naming the differing sources and the export command (V4 only for --full, which
runs the live V4 planners). An explicit variable is kept and must match too.
The receipt records each publication used and why.
Receipts name every exclusion; compiled test inventories reject dead exclusion
IDs. --slow separately adds the explicit ignored CPU soaks and is also required at release cuts. Install cargo-nextest
without root into the persistent architecture Cargo home (see AGENTS.md).
The gate finds it on PATH or there automatically; cargo test remains the fallback.
When cargo or rustc is not on PATH (a systemd user unit), the gate prepends the
existing standard Cargo bin directories ($CARGO_HOME/bin, ~/.cargo/bin, the
persistent architecture Cargo home, Homebrew's bin, and the toolchain rustup
resolves when /opt/rustup exists) for itself and every child before refusing.
Systemd user units inherit the user manager's groups, so Docker-socket tests
such as test_cleanup_foreign_root_refused reach /var/run/docker.sock when the
user is in the docker group. Don't pass -p SupplementaryGroups=docker: a user
manager can't set supplementary groups and the unit fails with 216/GROUP.

With none of --cargo, --scripts, --benches the first two run. --benches runs
the release host-cost bounds (BENCHES: the draft policy's round median at C16
under CUTEAFD_POLICY_BENCH_MAX_US) as their own suite,
recording each bench's medians. Everything lives under
~/.cache/cuteafd/builds/NAME (asserted build filesystem): the Cargo target
(fresh target-cpu-gate-kache/plain directories), TMPDIR and
pytest's basetemp. Submodules are initialized at their pinned commits first.
Compiling (cargo test --no-run) holds a build slot (scripts/build/build-slot.sh,
slot 0 is build.lock); running the tests holds none. Each suite has its own
timeout. The python is the checkout's .venv, else ~/.cache/cuteafd/gate-venv,
else the main checkout's .venv, verified against scripts/dev/gate-requirements.txt.

The verdict comes from the parsed test summaries, never from an exit code
alone: a suite passes only when its summaries report more than zero tests and
no failures. Failing test ids are listed per suite. --base REV runs the same
gate on a detached worktree of REV and reports only the ids that are new in
this checkout. --skip-live-planners explicitly excludes both live V4 and installed
checkpoint planners and records that choice. Missing or stale compiler inputs
fail the requested full gate; provenance must match the fork and export sources.

Writes NAME/cpu-gate.json and NAME/cpu-gate.md; exit 0 only when every
requested suite passed (or, with --base, introduced no new failing ids).
Both list the largest scratch consumers (bytes allocated under TMPDIR when the
suites end, grouped by pytest test or by top-level entry). The scratch (tmp/)
and the Cargo targets (tens of GB per run) are deleted on every exit, failure
and interrupt included; --keep leaves them for inspection. Logs and receipts
stay.
"""
